<section class="faq paint edge edge-b" id="faq" aria-labelledby="faq-title" data-shot="press">
  <div class="wrap faq-grid">
    <header class="faq-head">
      <p class="label kicker">FAQ</p>
      <h2 class="poster" id="faq-title" data-reveal>Fair questions.</h2>
      <p class="sub" data-reveal style="--i: 1">Short answers. All of them true.</p>
    </header>

    <dl class="qa">
      <div class="item">
        <dt>What does it cost?</dt>
        <dd>Nothing. It’s open source under GPL-3.0. No paid tier, no catch.</dd>
      </div>
      <div class="item" id="install">
        <dt>Is it safe to open?</dt>
        <dd>Signed and notarized by Apple, so macOS opens it without a fuss. The source is on GitHub if you’d rather check yourself.</dd>
      </div>
      <div class="item">
        <dt>What does it collect?</dt>
        <dd>Once a day the app sends one anonymous “opened today” count with its version and macOS version and no persistent ID, so we know how many people use it. Update checks are counted by version and day, and whether it’s a new copy’s first. Each launch also reads one small file, the same for everyone, so we can switch off a speed-up that misbehaves. Nothing else, unless you tick the box. Then crash reports and counts, never your sites or searches.</dd>
      </div>
      <div class="item">
        <dt>And this website?</dt>
        <dd>It counts its own visitors on its own servers, replays included, typing masked, gone after 30 days. No third party does the counting.</dd>
      </div>
      <div class="item">
        <dt>Coming from Arc?</dt>
        <dd>Same sidebar, same muscle memory. The importer brings your spaces, pinned tabs and their names along.</dd>
      </div>
      <div class="item">
        <dt>Is this Dia?</dt>
        <dd>No. It dresses like Dia, or like Arc if you ask.</dd>
      </div>
      <div class="item">
        <dt>Why “Ardia”?</dt>
        <dd>Arc plus Dia.</dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </div>
</section>

